"Everything in the heavens and on earth constantly glorifies Allah. This glorification demonstrates that sovereignty and praise belong to Him alone, and that He is capable of all things. Serving as an introduction to matters such as death and resurrection, this verse proclaims the truths that the disbelievers fail to comprehend.\n\nAllah created you; yet some among you are disbelievers, and some are believers. Both disbelief and faith are by Allah's creation, but the servant's will and choice play a role in this creation. The verse reflects both fatalistic and moderate views; in contrast to the Jabriyyah, the responsibility of the servant is emphasized. Allah sees what you do and requites everyone according to their deeds.\n\nAllah, who created the heavens and the earth in truth, fashioned man in the finest form. As a requirement of this noble creation, man ought to choose goodness and turn toward Allah. The return is to Allah, and everyone will receive the recompense for what they have done.\n\nAllah knows whatever is in the heavens and on earth; everything open and hidden is encompassed by His knowledge. The catastrophes that befell past nations show that deniers are punished in this world. To those who suppose they will not be resurrected, it is said: \"Nay, by my Lord, you will surely be resurrected.\"\n\nThat day is the Day of Taghabun (mutual loss and gain); the day when mutual deception will be brought to light, and profit and loss will become manifest. Those who believe and do righteous deeds will enter Paradise to abide therein eternally; while those who disbelieve will suffer eternal punishment in Hell. That indeed is the ultimate triumph.\n\nNo calamity strikes except by Allah's leave. Whoever believes in Allah, Allah guides his heart to what is right. Obey Allah and obey the Prophet; if you turn away, the messenger's duty is only to convey the message clearly. There is no deity except Allah; let the believers rely upon Him alone.\n\nAmong your spouses and your children are enemies to you; so beware of them. If you pardon and overlook, Allah is also forgiving. Your wealth and your children are a trial; the immense reward is with Allah. Fear Allah as much as you are able, obey, and spend. Whoever is saved from the stinginess of his own soul—they are the ones who attain success. If you lend Allah a goodly loan, He will multiply it manifold and forgive you. Allah knows the seen and the unseen; He is the All-Mighty, the All-Wise.\n\nNo calamity befalls except by Allah's permission. Whether one is a disbeliever or a believer, every kind of hardship that befalls anyone is by Allah's leave. Even though some calamities stem from a person's own self, they still do not occur without Allah's decree and permission. Allah guides the heart of whoever believes in Him, consoling him by reminding him that the calamity comes from Him and will return to Him. The patient are granted their reward without measure. Therefore, believe in Allah, His Messenger, and the Quran. Obey Allah, and obey the Prophet.\n\nO you who believe! Among your spouses and your children are enemies to you; so beware of them. This verse was revealed regarding some Muslims who wished to emigrate to Medina but were prevented by their spouses and children. Spouses and children, intentionally or unintentionally, may hold a person back from good deeds and even harm religion. For this reason, the man as the head of the family should be vigilant toward his family, raise them with religious upbringing, and not spoil them with excessive affection and indulgence. Along with this, he should pardon, overlook, and show tolerance toward their shortcomings. Allah is indeed the Forgiver of sins and the Merciful.\n\nYour wealth and your children are a fitnah (a trial and a source of hardship). By binding you to themselves, they may keep you away from righteous deeds and lead you into sin. Yet the great reward is with Allah. For this reason, one should prefer love for Allah, His remembrance, and obedience to Him over love for wealth and children; and while attending to concerns regarding wealth and children, one should not compromise the worship and obedience due to Allah.\n\nFear Allah as much as you are able, and hold to the path of piety (taqwa). Hear the commands, obey, and spend. Give zakah and charity from the provision Allah has granted you. Do good for your own souls. Seek refuge in Allah, and guard yourselves against greed and stinginess. Those who are preserved from the stinginess of their souls are the ones who achieve salvation. Do not forget Allah and fall behind in doing good out of anxiety over wealth and children.\n\nIf you lend Allah a goodly loan (spend your wealth for the sake of Allah), Allah will multiply its reward for you and forgive your sins. Allah is Shakur (the Most Appreciative, who rewards goodness manifold) and Halim (the Forbearing, who does not hasten to punish). He is the All-Mighty (Aziz) and the All-Wise (Hakim). He knows both the unseen (ghayb) and the seen (shahadah); He knows the secret and the manifest. When He wills, His reckoning is swift, yet He often grants respite. There is wisdom in everything He does.\n\nAt the end of the surah, a warning is issued regarding family matters: spouses and children can sometimes be enemies, so beware of them. The marital bond can be dissolved, but the parent-child bond is enduring. Through this wisdom, divorce was made permissible, and this surah is followed by Surah at-Talaq."